{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% macro choose(name, options, filled, label, help, blank) %}
    <label class="field">
        <span class="field-label">{{ label }}</span>
        <select name="{{ name }}">
            {% if blank is not null %}<option value="">{{ blank }}</option>{% endif %}
            {% for option in options %}
                {% set value = option.name is defined ? option.name : option %}
                {% set title = option.describe is defined ? option.describe : value %}
                <option value="{{ value }}" title="{{ title }}" {{ filled[name]|default('') == value ? 'selected' : '' }}>{{ value }}</option>
            {% endfor %}
        </select>
        {% if help %}<span class="field-help">{{ help }}</span>{% endif %}
    </label>
{% endmacro %}

{% macro check(name, filled, label, help) %}
    <label class="field check">
        <input type="checkbox" name="{{ name }}" value="on" {{ filled[name]|default(false) ? 'checked' : '' }}>
        <span class="field-label">{{ label }}</span>
        {% if help %}<span class="field-help">{{ help }}</span>{% endif %}
    </label>
{% endmacro %}

{% block title %}The forge of clients{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
    {% import _self as f %}

    <h1>Forge a client</h1>
    <p class="lead">One provider, one identity, and as many clients as there are ways of asking. Every
        choice below is read from the document <code>{{ provider_uri }}</code> publishes: what it does not
        announce is not offered here. The client is registered the moment you build it (RFC 7591), signs
        the user in, and deletes itself when you are done with it (RFC 7592).</p>

    {% if not choices.reachable %}
        <div class="card error">
            <h2>The document of this provider could not be read</h2>
            <p>Nothing was obtained from <code>{{ provider_uri }}/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>,
                so the form below offers nothing: that is this page failing to read the provider and not
                the provider offering nothing. Check that it is up, then reload.</p>
        </div>
    {% elseif not choices.registers %}
        <div class="card error">
            <h2>This provider registers no client</h2>
            <p>Its document announces no <code>registration_endpoint</code>, so nothing can be built here.
                A tenant decides that, and its mode is written in its configuration.</p>
        </div>
    {% elseif not holds_a_token %}
        <div class="card error">
            <h2>This demonstration holds no initial access token</h2>
            <p>The registration endpoint of this tenant asks for one (RFC 7591 §1.2), and the console of
                the provider issues it: <code>castor demo --forge</code> writes one into
                <code>.env.local</code> as <code>FORGE_INITIAL_ACCESS_TOKEN</code>. Building a client
                without one is refused by the provider, and the page will say so in its words.</p>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    {% if deleted %}
        <div class="card ok" data-deleted="{{ deleted }}" data-answered="{{ answered }}">
            <h2>The client <code>{{ deleted }}</code> deleted itself</h2>
            <p>It asked the provider to remove its registration at the URI it was handed, with the
                credential it was handed, and the provider answered <code>{{ answered }}</code>
                (RFC 7592 §2.3 answers 204 and no body).</p>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    {% if error %}
        <div class="card error">
            <h2>The provider refused this composition</h2>
            <p><code>{{ error }}</code></p>
        </div>
    {% endif %}

    <h2>Presets</h2>
    <p class="muted">{{ presets|length }} compositions written down. A preset fills the form below, and building
        it replaces the client of the same name.</p>
    <div class="presets">
        {% for preset in presets %}
            <div class="card preset">
                <h2>{{ preset.name }}</h2>
                <p>{{ preset.what }}</p>
                <p>
                    <a class="button" href="{{ path('app_forge', {preset: preset.slug}) }}#compose">Fill the form</a>
                    <form class="inline" method="post" action="{{ path('app_forge_build') }}">
                        <input type="hidden" name="preset" value="{{ preset.slug }}">
                        <button class="button" type="submit">Build and sign in</button>
                    </form>
                </p>
            </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>

    {% if built is not empty %}
        <h2>Clients this browser has built</h2>
        <table class="kv">
            {% for client in built %}
                <tr>
                    <th><a href="{{ path('app_forge_profile', {handle: client.handle}) }}">{{ client.handle }}</a></th>
                    <td>
                        <code>{{ client.clientId }}</code> · {{ client.name }}
                        <form class="inline" method="post" action="{{ path('app_forge_delete', {handle: client.handle}) }}">
                            <button class="button" type="submit">Delete it</button>
                        </form>
                        <a href="{{ path('app_forge', {from: client.handle}) }}#compose">Take it apart</a>
                    </td>
                </tr>
            {% endfor %}
        </table>
    {% endif %}

    <h2 id="compose">Compose one</h2>
    <form class="compose" method="post" action="{{ path('app_forge_build') }}">
        {% if chosen_preset %}<p class="muted">Filled with the preset <code>{{ chosen_preset }}</code>. What
            is submitted is this form, so anything below can be changed before it is built; it will then be
            built under a handle of its own and leave the preset where it is.</p>{% endif %}

        <fieldset>
            <legend>What it is</legend>
            <label class="field">
                <span class="field-label">client_name</span>
                <input type="text" name="client_name" value="{{ filled.client_name|default('') }}" placeholder="What the consent page calls it">
                <span class="field-help">What the user reads on the consent page (RFC 7591 §2).</span>
            </label>
            {{ f.choose('subject_type', choices.subject_types, filled, 'subject_type', 'The kind of "sub" this client sees: the same identifier as everybody else, or one it alone gets (OIDC Core §8).', null) }}
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset>
            <legend>What it asks for</legend>
            <div class="scopes">
                <span class="field-label">scope</span>
                <span class="field-help"><code>openid</code> is in every composition, this being a client that signs a user in.</span>
                {% for scope in choices.scopes %}
                    <label class="check">
                        <input type="checkbox" name="scope[]" value="{{ scope }}" {{ scope in filled.scope|default([]) ? 'checked' : '' }}>
                        <code>{{ scope }}</code>
                    </label>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
            {{ f.choose('authorization_details_type', choices.authorization_details_types, filled, 'authorization_details type', 'What it may do, to what and up to how much, instead of a kind of thing (RFC 9396 §2). The provider reads the types it is wired with and no other.', 'none') }}
            <label class="field">
                <span class="field-label">authorization_details</span>
                <textarea name="authorization_details" rows="7" placeholder="[{&quot;type&quot;: ...}]">{{ filled.authorization_details|default('') }}</textarea>
                <span class="field-help">The JSON array this client sends at the authorization endpoint. The consent page says it in words.</span>
            </label>
            {{ f.choose('acr_values', choices.acr_values, filled, 'acr_values', 'The authentication this client asks for (OIDC Core §3.1.2.1): the provider answers with what it got, and a login that cannot reach it is refused.', 'whatever the provider does') }}
            {{ f.choose('prompt', choices.prompts, filled, 'prompt', 'What it asks of the interaction: a login even if a session is open, a consent again, an account to be chosen.', 'nothing in particular') }}
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset>
            <legend>How it proves it is itself</legend>
            {{ f.choose('token_endpoint_auth_method', choices.authentication, filled, 'token_endpoint_auth_method', 'What the forge draws for it follows: a secret the provider issues, a key it publishes, or a certificate it presents.', null) }}
            {% for method in choices.authentication %}
                <p class="field-help"><code>{{ method.name }}</code> — {{ method.describe }}</p>
            {% endfor %}
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset>
            <legend>How the request travels</legend>
            {{ f.choose('response_mode', choices.response_modes, filled, 'response_mode', null, null) }}
            {% for mode in choices.response_modes %}
                <p class="field-help"><code>{{ mode.name }}</code> — {{ mode.describe }}</p>
            {% endfor %}
            {{ f.check('pushed', filled, 'Push the request (RFC 9126)', 'The request is handed to the provider server to server, and the browser carries a client identifier and a URI.') }}
            {{ f.check('require_pushed_authorization_requests', filled, 'Register that it always pushes', 'The provider then refuses a request of this client that was not pushed.') }}
            {{ f.choose('request_object_signing_alg', choices.request_object_algorithms, filled, 'request_object_signing_alg', 'The request is signed as a request object (RFC 9101 §4), which the forge signs with the key it drew.', 'no request object') }}
            {{ f.check('require_signed_request_object', filled, 'Register that it always signs its request', 'The provider then reads a request of this client from a signature and from nothing else.') }}
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset>
            <legend>What comes back</legend>
            {{ f.choose('authorization_signed_response_alg', choices.documents.authorization.signing, filled, 'authorization_signed_response_alg', 'JARM §3: the answer is one signed parameter. A signed response mode above takes this one.', 'what the provider signs with') }}
            {{ f.choose('authorization_encrypted_response_alg', choices.documents.authorization.encryption, filled, 'authorization_encrypted_response_alg', 'And encrypted to a key the forge draws and publishes for it.', 'not encrypted') }}
            {{ f.choose('id_token_signed_response_alg', choices.documents.id_token.signing, filled, 'id_token_signed_response_alg', null, 'what the provider signs with') }}
            {{ f.choose('id_token_encrypted_response_alg', choices.documents.id_token.encryption, filled, 'id_token_encrypted_response_alg', 'OIDC Core §3.1.3.7: the ID token arrives as a JWE holding a JWS.', 'not encrypted') }}
            {{ f.choose('userinfo_signed_response_alg', choices.documents.userinfo.signing, filled, 'userinfo_signed_response_alg', 'OIDC Core §5.3.2: the UserInfo answer becomes an "application/jwt" instead of JSON.', 'plain JSON') }}
            {{ f.choose('userinfo_encrypted_response_alg', choices.documents.userinfo.encryption, filled, 'userinfo_encrypted_response_alg', null, 'not encrypted') }}
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset>
            <legend>What it receives, and how it holds it</legend>
            {{ f.choose('token_binding', choices.bindings, filled, 'token binding', null, null) }}
            {% for binding in choices.bindings %}
                <p class="field-help"><code>{{ binding.name }}</code> — {{ binding.describe }}</p>
            {% endfor %}
            {{ f.choose('dpop_signing_alg', choices.dpop_algorithms, filled, 'dpop_signing_alg', 'What the proofs of this login are signed with, for the binding to a key.', 'ES256') }}
            {{ f.check('tls_client_certificate_bound_access_tokens', filled, 'Register that its tokens are bound to its certificate', 'RFC 8705 §3.4, for a client that presents one without authenticating with it.') }}
        </fieldset>

        <fieldset>
            <legend>What a logout reaches</legend>
            {{ f.check('frontchannel_logout', filled, 'Front-Channel Logout 1.0', 'The logout page of the provider loads a URI of this client in a frame.') }}
            {{ f.check('backchannel_logout', filled, 'Back-Channel Logout 1.0', 'The provider posts a logout token to this client, wherever the session ended.') }}
            {{ f.check('logout_session_required', filled, 'And it is told which session ended', 'The provider then names the session in what it sends (§2.2 and §3).') }}
        </fieldset>

        <p><button class="button" type="submit">Build it and sign in</button></p>
    </form>

    <h2>What a client cannot ask for</h2>
    <p class="muted">Seven members of this provider are not offered above, and that is the rule and not an
        omission: they are written by whoever declares a client in the configuration, or by the
        registration policy of the tenant, and a client that registers itself is refused rather than
        obeyed. A client that comes from nowhere gets nothing a declared client does not get by asking for
        it (decision 0009).</p>
    <table class="kv">
        {% for member, what in choices.written_by_the_deployment %}
            <tr><th><code>{{ member }}</code></th><td>{{ what }}</td></tr>
        {% endfor %}
    </table>
    <p class="muted">So the shape of the access token is the tenant's, and the page of a built client reads
        it off the token it was issued rather than off what it asked for. The two shapes are seen on the
        clients the configuration declares, which <code>castor demo --resource-server</code> signs in
        as.</p>

    <h2>What has no browser</h2>
    <p class="muted">Some flows of the provider are never clicked, because no browser of this application
        goes into them: the client is a program, or it is the provider that calls the client back. They are
        played from the command line of the provider's repository, <code>castor demo --device</code>,
        <code>--ciba</code>, <code>--service</code>, <code>--assertion</code>, <code>--native</code> and
        <code>--push</code>, and each one asserts every step. The forge composes a client of the
        authorization code grant, which is the one a browser plays.</p>
{% endblock %}
